From d191144b805a025398c6b2bf198ce10f9ccf9728 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 13:46:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AppKit: add a Wayland backend
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cocotron loads display backends as .backend bundles chosen by NSPriority, and
X11 was the only one. This adds Wayland: a WaylandDisplay/WaylandWindow pair
that renders through wl_shm and xdg-shell, plus WaylandInput translating
wl_keyboard/wl_pointer into NSEvent.

The pixel path needs no conversion. Onyx2D renders into an O2Surface tagged
kO2ImageAlphaPremultipliedFirst | kO2BitmapByteOrder32Little, which is the same
32-bit little-endian ARGB word as WL_SHM_FORMAT_ARGB8888, so flushBuffer is a
memcpy into a shm pool followed by attach/damage/commit. Two buffers per window
are tracked through wl_buffer.release so the compositor is never handed a
surface that is still being drawn into.

Unlike X11.backend this does not route the software-rendered frame through GLX;
that indirection is an artefact of the X11 path, not a requirement of the
raster one.

Deliberately unimplemented rather than stubbed: -deminiaturize/-isMiniaturized
raise the inherited abstract-invocation error, because xdg-shell's toplevel
state enum has no minimised value and no un-minimise request — a silent no-op
would be a worse lie than a loud failure.

Known gaps, all commented at their sites: damage is always full-frame because
neither Cocotron nor the X11 backend gave a verified answer about which
coordinate space a partial rect would arrive in, and guessing it wrong would
corrupt the display; -screens falls back to 1024x768 until a wl_output.mode
event arrives; the waits for the first xdg_surface.configure and for a buffer
release have no timeout.

Nothing here has been compiled. The build prerequisites are present on the
target, though: wayland 1.25, wayland-protocols 1.47, libxkbcommon 1.13,
wayland-scanner, and xdg-shell.xml under /usr/local/share/wayland-protocols —
checked on the FreeBSD dev machine, not in the authoring sandbox, where their
absence had been flagged as a risk.
